# PID 1's whole program. It is part of the initramfs and therefore part of the
# measured launch, so it is meant to be read in full — there is nothing here
# that is not on this page.
#
# busybox init execs these directly; none contain shell metacharacters, which
# matters because the image has no shell to fall back on.

# The pseudo-filesystems the runtime needs before /bin/app starts.
#
# /dev is not a formality: /dev/sev-guest is where attestation reports come
# from, and engine-supervisor points each disposable child's stdout and stderr
# at /dev/null so an escaped child cannot write a round's plaintext to a
# descriptor it inherited. Inheritance is not the only route, though — every
# node here has a name, so the same child's seccomp filter also refuses to open
# anything for writing.
#
# /tmp and /run are tmpfs because the CVM has no disk — every writable path is
# encrypted RAM. Both are noexec: nothing here ever executes out of a writable
# path, and refusing up front costs nothing.
::sysinit:/bin/busybox mount -t proc -o nosuid,nodev,noexec proc /proc
::sysinit:/bin/busybox mount -t sysfs -o nosuid,nodev,noexec sysfs /sys
::sysinit:/bin/busybox mount -t devtmpfs -o nosuid devtmpfs /dev
::sysinit:/bin/busybox mount -t tmpfs -o nosuid,nodev,noexec tmpfs /tmp
::sysinit:/bin/busybox mount -t tmpfs -o nosuid,nodev,noexec tmpfs /run

# The application. Each role's initramfs provides its own binary under this one
# name, so PID 1 needs no configuration and there is nothing to mis-set.
::wait:/bin/app

# When the application ends, the machine ends. This runs whether it exited
# cleanly or died, which is the behaviour we want: there is no in-place
# recovery, and a CVM with nothing left to do should stop existing rather than
# sit idle holding a session's memory.
::once:/bin/busybox poweroff -f
